  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1148 PM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nTemperatures will be warmer Sunday as a shortwave upper ridge moves \neast over Texas. May see some breaks in the clouds in the afternoon, \nbut skies overall are expected to stay mostly cloudy with moist \nsoutherly low-level flow. Expect highs to be the upper 70s to lower \n80s in our northern and central counties, and mid to upper 70s \nfarther south. \n\nA large scale upper low and trough will move east into the western \nCONUS Sunday, and overspread the Rockies on Monday. A low is progged \nto develop in the southern portion of this trough over northern New \nMexico, and then track across the Texas Panhandle and lift to the \nKansas/Oklahoma border Monday night. Cold front associated with this \nsystem is forecast to move southeast across our area Monday night. \nThe track of the upper low and trough will be important for rain\nchances late Monday and Monday night. A track a little farther to\nthe north would, for the most part, result in dry slotting for \nour area. At this time, appears that there will be enough lift, \nmoisture and instability for a band of showers and thunderstorms \nto develop along the cold front, as it pushes across our area. The\nhigher rain chances and better coverage of convection look to be \nin our eastern and southeastern counties where more moisture\nshould be available. Should also mention that SPC has the northern\nBig Country in a Day 4 Slight Risk of severe storms primarily for\nlate Monday afternoon and evening. \n\nCooler air will follow this frontal passage and will be accompanied \nby breezy north-northwest winds on Tuesday. After a warm day Monday \nwith highs in the upper 70s to mid 80s, highs Tuesday will be in the \nlower to mid 70s. May see some wrap around cloud cover over the \nnortheast part of our area on Tuesday, as moisture wraps around the \ndeparting upper system. Dry weather to follow for Wednesday and \nThursday, as surface high pressure settles into Texas by Wednesday \nand shifts to the east on Thursday. Dry weather to continue Friday\nwith south winds returning. After the period of well-above normal\nmorning low temperatures our area has seen in the past several \ndays, will have a couple of cool nights Tuesday night and \nWednesday night with lows in the 40s. Daytime temperatures look \npleasant Wednesday into Friday with forecast highs in the 70s.",
